<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maria Montessori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maria Montessori was an Italian physician and educator who developed the Montessori method of teaching. She opened her first Casa dei Bambini (Children's House) in a working-class district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1907, basing it on children's self-directed activity with specially designed materials. Her method spread worldwide; she left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> in the 1930s and spent her later years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maria-montessori.jpg" alt="Maria Montessori" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maria_Montessori_(portrait).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maria Tecla Artemisia Montessori was born on 31 August 1870 in Chiaravalle, in the province of Ancona. Her father, Alessandro Montessori, was an official of the Ministry of Finance working in the local state tobacco factory; her mother, Renilde Stoppani, was well educated for the time and a niece of the geologist Antonio Stoppani. The family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1875.</p><p>She first enrolled at an all-boys technical school, hoping to become an engineer, then entered medical school at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapienza-university-of-rome">Sapienza University of Rome</a> as one of the first women to study medicine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and graduated with honours in 1896. From 1896 to 1901 she researched children then described as phrenasthenic, in modern terms children with some form of cognitive delay or disability, and became an advocate for women's rights and for the education of children with learning difficulties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1902 Montessori enrolled in the philosophy course at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which then included much of what is now psychology, and did anthropological research with Italian schoolchildren. She lectured at the university's Pedagogic School until 1908. In 1906 she was invited to oversee the care of children of working parents in a new apartment building for low-income families in the San Lorenzo district of Rome.</p><p>The first Casa dei Bambini opened on 6 January 1907 with 50 or 60 children aged between two or three and six or seven. Montessori observed while the building porter's daughter did the daily teaching. By the end of 1911 Montessori education had been adopted in public schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and by 1912 schools had opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and many other Western European cities. In 1916 she moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and for twenty years taught training courses across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1946 she returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, and she died of a cerebral haemorrhage on 6 May 1952 in Noordwijk aan Zee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Montessori drew on Jean Marc Gaspard Itard, Édouard Séguin, Friedrich Fröbel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-heinrich-pestalozzi">Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi</a>. Her method rests on observing children free to act in an environment prepared to meet their needs. She concluded that their spontaneous activity revealed an internal programme of development and that the educator's role was to remove obstacles to it. She described four planes of development, from birth to six, six to twelve, twelve to eighteen and eighteen to twenty-four, and developed methods and materials for the first two. Her major books include The Montessori Method, in English in 1912.</p><p>The name Montessori is not trademarked, so a range of practices exists under it. Common elements are mixed-age classrooms, free choice of activity, long uninterrupted work periods and specially trained teachers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maria Montessori” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culturally the period produced the operas of Giuseppe Verdi and Giacomo Puccini, the Futurist movement, Guglielmo Marconi’s radio and the educational method of <b>Maria Montessori</b>, while the racial laws of 1938 drove scientists such as Enrico Fermi into exile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chiaravalle, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Noordwijk, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.6,13.33&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Chiaravalle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Maria Montessori"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5818 3800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5818 3800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.6,13.33&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
